          dealing: "Deal all cards face down, 8 per player, proceeding anticlockwise. The dealer flips the bottom card of their hand to set the trump suit (if it's an Over-Knave, they keep flipping).",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Collect as many Aces and Tens as possible. There are 8 total points (4 Aces, 4 Tens).',
          gameplay: "1. Lead: Player to dealer's right leads.\n2. Follow Suit: You must follow suit if possible.\n3. Mandatory Trumping: If you cannot follow suit, you MUST play a trump if you have one. If you have neither the suit nor trumps, you may discard any card.\n4. Over-Knaves: These always belong to the trump suit, regardless of their printed suit logo.",
          keyRules: [
            'No Passing: You cannot hold back a trump if you are void in the led suit.',
            'Anticlockwise: All play and dealing moves to the right.',
            'Winning condition: Collect as many Aces and Tens as possible.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Keep track of the four Over-Knaves. If you hold the 'Acorn' Over-Knave, you have the absolute highest card in the gameâ€”save it to capture an opponent's Ace or Ten.",
          intermediate: "The Tenth Order: Because Tens are worth exactly as much as Aces (1 point), treat them with equal value. Throwing a Ten on your partner's winning trick is a common and necessary strategy.",
          advanced: "Void-Forcing: If you know an opponent is void in a suit, lead that suit repeatedly to force them to use up their high trumps (Over-Knaves) early, 'cleaning out' the defense for your Aces later in the game.",
          tips: [
            'In the first few tricks, try to extract the Over-Knaves from the opponents to see where the power lies.',
            'If your partner leads an Ace, throw your Ten of that suit on top of it immediately to secure the points.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
